/-!
# Regular sequences are quasi-regular (Stacks tags 00LN, 061M)

Throughout, `R` is a commutative unital ring, `M` is an `R`-module, and
`f_1, …, f_c ∈ R` is a finite sequence of elements of `R`. We write
`J = (f_1, …, f_c) ⊆ R` for the ideal generated by the sequence.

For a multi-index `I = (i_1, …, i_c) ∈ ℤ_{≥0}^c` we write `|I| = i_1 + … + i_c`
and `f^I = f_1^{i_1} ⋯ f_c^{i_c} ∈ R`.

## Main Definitions

* **Definition 1 (`M`-regular sequence, Tag 00LF / Def 10.68.1).**
  `f_1, …, f_c` is an *`M`-regular sequence* if:
  1. for each `i`, `f_i` is a nonzerodivisor on `M / (f_1, …, f_{i-1}) M`; and
  2. `M / (f_1, …, f_c) M` is nonzero.
  When `M = R` this is simply a *regular sequence*.

  This is exactly Mathlib's `RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular M rs`, whose two
  fields `regular_mod_prev` and `top_ne_smul` are precisely conditions (1) and
  (2) above.

* **Definition 2 (canonical comparison map, eq. 10.69.0.1 / Tag 061M).**
  There is a canonical graded `(R/J)[X_1,…,X_c]`-module homomorphism
  `Φ_M : (M/JM) ⊗_{R/J} (R/J)[X_1,…,X_c] → ⊕_{n ≥ 0} J^n M / J^{n+1} M`,
  sending `m̄ ⊗ X_1^{e_1} ⋯ X_c^{e_c}` to `f^E m mod J^{|E|+1} M`, where the
  degree-`n` part of the source is spanned by the `m̄ ⊗ X^I` with `|I| = n`.
  This map is always well defined and surjective.

* **Definition 3 (`M`-quasi-regular sequence, Tag 061M / Def 10.69.1).**
  `f_1, …, f_c` is *`M`-quasi-regular* iff `Φ_M` is an isomorphism, equivalently
  (as `Φ_M` is always surjective) iff `Φ_M` is injective. When `M = R` this is
  simply a *quasi-regular sequence*.

  **Reformulation used here (and in the proof).** Since `Φ_M` is homogeneous of
  degree `0` and always surjective, injectivity of `Φ_M` is equivalent to
  injectivity of each degree-`n` component, which unwinds to: for every `n ≥ 0`
  and every family `(m_I)_{|I| = n}` of elements of `M`,
    `∑_{|I| = n} f^I m_I ∈ J^{n+1} M  ⟹  m_I ∈ J M  for all I with |I| = n`.
  We take this elementary statement as the Lean definition of quasi-regularity;
  the source explicitly endorses it as equivalent to Definition 3 and as the
  concrete property the proof establishes. (For `M = R` it reads: any relation
  `∑_{|I|=n} a_I f^I ∈ J^{n+1}` with `a_I ∈ R` forces `a_I ∈ J`.)

## Main Statements

* **Statement 1.** A regular sequence of `R` is a quasi-regular sequence
  (the special case `M = R` of Statement 2).
* **Statement 2.** If `f_1, …, f_c` is an `M`-regular sequence, then it is an
  `M`-quasi-regular sequence.

## Notes

* Condition (2) (`M/JM ≠ 0`) of the regular-sequence definition is not used in
  the proof; we nevertheless carry the full Definition 1 (`IsRegular`) as the
  hypothesis, which is the most faithful rendering.
* Indexing: with a list `rs : List R` of length `c`, a multi-index is a function
  `I : Fin rs.length → ℕ`, `|I| = ∑ i, I i`, and `f^I = ∏ i, rs[i] ^ (I i)`.
  The finset of multi-indices of total degree `n` is `Finset.Nat.antidiagonalTuple`.
* `J^n M` is rendered as `(Ideal.ofList rs ^ n) • (⊤ : Submodule R M)` and
  membership "`m ∈ J M`" as `m ∈ (Ideal.ofList rs) • (⊤ : Submodule R M)`.
-/
